BT 1 0 0 1 0 243.804 Tm .192 Tw 18 TL /F2 12 Tf .066667 .066667 .066667 rg (A: Yes. The HMO is doing an insurance business in substance and is answerable to regulation as) Tj T* 0 Tw (an insurer.) Tj  T* .792857 Tw (Under Section 2\(b\)\(1\) of the Insurance Code, making insurance contracts as insurer is doing an) Tj T* 0 Tw .451 Tw (insurance business, and under Section 2\(b\)\(4\), any business in substance equivalent to insurance) Tj T* 0 Tw .004 Tw (that is designed to evade the Code is caught. The label on the card does not control. The principal) Tj T* 0 Tw .263333 Tw (object and purpose test decides. By contrast, a pre-need plan is held to its literal terms as the law) Tj T* 0 Tw .428 Tw (between the parties rather than treated as Section 2\(a\) indemnity \(Gaw v. CA, G.R. No. 147748,) Tj T* 0 Tw (April 19, 2006\).) Tj  T* .444 Tw (Here, the HMO assumes risk for a price. The fixed fee is the consideration. Epidemic overuse is) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.361143 Tw (the contingent fortuitous cost. The guarantee of doctor visits is the undertaking to answer that) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.033846 Tw (contingency. The counter-reading that the HMO merely sells access to services fails because it) Tj T* 0 Tw (absorbs cost variance instead of charging per visit.) Tj  T* 2.675077 Tw (The HMO is therefore transacting insurance business in substance. It needs a certificate of) Tj T* 0 Tw (authority, and operating without one is punishable.) Tj T* ET
