BT 1 0 0 1 0 297.804 Tm 1.101882 Tw 18 TL /F2 12 Tf .066667 .066667 .066667 rg (A: The claim is sustainable and every one of the four denial grounds fails, so the insurer must) Tj T* 0 Tw (pay the full value of the totally lost log shipment.) Tj  T* 2.54775 Tw (Under Sec. 86 of the Insurance Code, the insurer is liable where the insured peril was the) Tj T* 0 Tw .59025 Tw (proximate cause of the loss even if an uncontemplated peril was a remote cause, and under Sec.) Tj T* 0 Tw .13125 Tw (89 negligence of the insured or its agents never exonerates while only a willful act or connivance) Tj T* 0 Tw .201882 Tw (bars recovery, which is why a sunken cargo rendered valueless to its owner is an actual total loss) Tj T* 0 Tw .076286 Tw (payable in full without notice of abandonment \(Pan Malayan Insurance Corp. v. CA \(FAO\), G.R.) Tj T* 0 Tw (No. 95070, 1991\).) Tj  T* .4264 Tw (Here, ATC bought an all-risks marine policy for logs carried by Andok Shipping, and the vessel) Tj T* 0 Tw .667765 Tw (sank in a strong typhoon so the shipment was totally lost, which makes the typhoon peril of the) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.88025 Tw (sea the proximate cause of the loss. The first three grounds go to an unseaworthy vessel, an) Tj T* 0 Tw .792 Tw (untrained crew, and one-sided loading, and each sounds in negligence that Sec. 89 keeps inside) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.3616 Tw (coverage rather than in a willful act that would bar it. The fourth ground concedes fortuity) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.341231 Tw (because extremely bad weather proves the loss was fortuitous, and no specially excepted peril) Tj T* 0 Tw (was invoked under the Sec. 88 but-for test.) Tj  T* .52575 Tw (The denial is therefore improper and the insurer is liable for the total loss, with ATC recovering) Tj T* 0 Tw (the full insured value of the shipment.) Tj T* ET
