BT 1 0 0 1 0 315.804 Tm 1.244571 Tw 18 TL /F2 12 Tf .066667 .066667 .066667 rg (A: The hull claim fails for losses flowing from the unrepaired defect because seaworthiness is) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.112923 Tw (reckoned at every voyage and unreasonable delay in repair exonerates the insurer, while the) Tj T* 0 Tw (cargo claim is tested separately on cargo fitness.) Tj  T* 4.442769 Tw (Under Sec. 117\(a\) of the Insurance Code, a time policy demands seaworthiness at the) Tj T* 0 Tw .454 Tw (commencement of every voyage undertaken during the period, and under Sec. 120 unreasonable) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.724308 Tw (delay in repairing unseaworthiness exonerates the insurer on resulting loss. Under Sec. 121 a) Tj T* 0 Tw .648857 Tw (ship seaworthy for hull purposes may be unseaworthy for cargo purposes, a divide the Supreme) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.766571 Tw (Court enforced in Go Tiaoco v. Union Insurance Society of Canton \(G.R. No. 13983, 1919\),) Tj T* 0 Tw .17625 Tw (where a pre-voyage defect and slovenly repair made seawater entry a peril of the ship rather than) Tj T* 0 Tw (of the sea.) Tj  T* .132 Tw (Here, the unreasonable delay of the master in repairing the known defect triggers Sec. 120 on the) Tj T* 0 Tw .883636 Tw (resulting hull loss, so inception-period seaworthiness cannot save the mid-period voyage, while) Tj T* 0 Tw .080667 Tw (the recovery of the cargo owner turns on whether the vessel was fit for that cargo at the inception) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.775692 Tw (of the risk. The counter-reading that one seaworthiness finding covers both hull and cargo) Tj T* 0 Tw (collapses against Sec. 121, which measures them independently.) Tj  T* 1.54725 Tw (The denial is therefore proper on the resulting hull loss and cargo recovery stands or falls on) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.8915 Tw (cargo-specific unseaworthiness, because voyage-stage fitness and subject-specific fitness each) Tj T* 0 Tw (failed or must each be proved.) Tj T* ET
